Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be an irreducible affine variety over an algebraically closed field $k$ with coordinate ring $A=k[X]$ and function field $k(X)$. Let $B$ be the integral closure of $A$ in $k(X)$; it is a finite $A$-module and a normal domain. The normalization of $X$ is the affine variety $X^{
u}$ with $k[X^{
u}]\cong B$, together with the finite morphism $
u:X^{
u}	o X$ whose pullback is the inclusion $A\hookrightarrow B$. The pair $(X^{
u},
u)$ is unique up to unique isomorphism over $X$.

Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be an irreducible affine variety over an algebraically closed field and $
u:X^{
u}	o X$ its normalization. Then $
u$ is finite, surjective, and birational: it induces an isomorphism $k(X)\xrightarrow{\sim}k(X^{
u})$ of function fields over $k$.

Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be an irreducible classical variety over an algebraically closed field. Then $X$ has a normalization: a finite, surjective, birational morphism $
u:X^{
u}	o X$ from a normal variety $X^{
u}$, determined up to unique isomorphism over $X$. On each affine chart $U$ with coordinate ring $A$, $
u^{-1}(U)$ is the affine normalization of $U$; the charts glue by identities inside the common function field, and the construction is independent of the chosen finite affine cover. A reduced reducible variety is normalized by taking the disjoint union of the normalizations of its irreducible components.

Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be a classical variety with normalization $
u:X^{
u}	o X$ and let $U\subseteq X$ be a nonempty open subvariety. Then $
u^{-1}(U)$ is a normalization of $U$: it is normal, and $
u^{-1}(U)	o U$ is finite, surjective, and birational, so it is isomorphic to $U^{
u}$ over $U$.
